A loud error is annoying, but at least it tells you something is wrong.
A silent timeout is worse.
The user clicks a button. The spinner appears. Nothing happens. The browser waits. The server does not respond quickly enough. Maybe the request eventually fails. Maybe the frontend gives up. Maybe the user refreshes the page and tries again. Maybe the backend actually completed the operation, but the response never reached the client.
This is how duplicate orders, confused users, broken forms, and support tickets happen.
Silent timeouts are dangerous because they sit between success and failure. The system does not clearly tell the user what happened, and the developer may not immediately see the root cause.
