Silent timeouts create uncertainty for users and developers at the same time.
Timeouts can happen at many layers. The browser waits for a response. A CDN or proxy may have its own timeout. A load balancer may stop waiting. The application server may be busy. A database query may take too long. A third-party API may be slow. One timeout may trigger another.
That is why timeout handling should be designed intentionally.
The frontend should not wait forever. A request should have a reasonable limit, and the UI should explain what happened when that limit is reached.
async function fetchWithTimeout(
url: string,
options: RequestInit = {},
timeoutMs = 10000
) {
const controller = new AbortController();
const timeout = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), timeoutMs); try {
return await fetch(url, {
...options,
signal: controller.signal
});
} finally {
clearTimeout(timeout);
}
}Now the application can treat timeout as a real state, not as an invisible failure.
try {
const response = await fetchWithTimeout("/api/checkout", {
method: "POST",
body: JSON.stringify(payload)
});
} catch (error) {
showError("The request took too long. Please check before trying again.");
}That message should be written carefully. For some actions, especially payments or order creation, telling users to “try again” immediately can be risky. The operation may have succeeded on the server even though the client never received confirmation.
This is why idempotency and status checking matter. For critical actions, the client should send a unique operation ID.
await fetch("/api/orders", {
method: "POST",
headers: {
"Idempotency-Key": operationId
},
body: JSON.stringify(orderPayload)
});If the user retries, the server can recognize the same operation and avoid creating a duplicate order.
