Critical actions need timeout handling and idempotency together.
Server-side timeouts also need attention. A backend route should not perform slow operations without clear limits. Database queries should be monitored. Third-party API calls should have their own timeouts. Long-running work should often move into background jobs rather than blocking the user request.
For example, sending emails, generating large exports, syncing external systems, or processing media may not belong directly inside the request-response cycle. The API can accept the request, enqueue a job, and return a status that the frontend can poll or subscribe to.
{
"jobId": "job_123",
"status": "processing"
}The frontend can then show progress instead of waiting forever.
Timeouts are not only technical failures. They are user experience failures. A user who does not know whether their action succeeded is more stressed than a user who sees a clear error. The goal is not to prevent every slow request forever. That is impossible. The goal is to make slow or uncertain states safe and understandable.
Observability helps too. Timeouts should be visible in logs and metrics. The team should know which routes are slow, which third-party services are causing delays, and whether timeouts increased after a deployment. Without monitoring, timeout issues often appear first as vague user complaints.
The best systems make failure obvious. They do not leave users staring at a spinner. They do not let duplicate operations happen silently. They do not hide slow dependencies behind a generic loading state.
A loud error can be fixed. A silent timeout can quietly damage trust.
